 # Loop Contract
+
+## OpenCode team host notes (kit patch, read first)
+
+This copy is adapted from hdkhosravian/loop-contract-skill (commit 9d13d9a). Differences from the text below:
+- Run state lives in `.opencode/loops/<job-slug>/` (the text says `.claude/loops`; read it as this path). Only `tech-lead` writes there; `developer` cannot.
+- Scripts run in place: `python3 ~/.config/opencode/skills/loop-contract/scripts/fold_ledger.py ...`. Never copy them into the project.
+- The workers are the team: each item is implemented as a task card by `developer` (or `developer-strong` for T2) with red-green per item, reviewed by `reviewer`, and facts come from `explore`. No other subagents; workers never spawn workers; writes stay single-threaded.
+- Task cards from this job use the normal card header and `scripts/board.sh`; the ledger adds the per-item proof, it does not replace the cards.
+- `ScheduleWakeup`, `CronCreate`, `/loop`, `/plugin` and Claude memory do not exist here. To pause: update `PROGRESS.md` Now and `INDEX.md`, say the run is PAUSED with counts, and tell the user to say "continue".
+- Triage step 0 routes only among the team's lanes (card, hard card, slice, this contract); skip surveys of plugins and skills.
+- Read `references/*` only when the branch needs them. Do not load all of them.
+- Serial only: wherever the text offers parallel items or parallel workers, choose serial (one writer at a time).
+- Workers (`developer`, `reviewer`, `explore`) return their result as text; only `tech-lead` writes ledger, INDEX and verdict files, so ledger rows come from the cards' reports and `git log`, not from workers.
+- `--oracle-cmd` / proof commands: use the project's `scripts/check.sh` (whole gate) or one specific test command; they run on the host with no extras.
+- Anywhere the text says `.claude/` (including references), read `.opencode/`. Loop state under `.opencode/loops/` is committed with the project (it is the audit trail); if it is gitignored in this repo, say so in the report.
+- Needs `python3` on the PATH (the setup script checks it); if it is missing, say so and fall back to the normal card lane.
 
